+# Agent Note: Model-input Host provenance and admission
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-30-model-input-host-provenance.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A Host composition may need to admit model inputs only from selected configured plugin rows. Prompt sections, dynamic contexts, and tool schemas already resolve through scoped shadowing, restrictions, dynamic providers, deterministic ordering, and the assembly waterfall, but the resulting values identify only what the model receives. They do not identify the Loader entry that registered each contribution.
+
+Package names are insufficient provenance because one composition may mount the same plugin more than once, replace a row while retaining its configured identity, or register scoped contributions below another row. Adding ownership fields to `PromptAssembly` or `ToolSchema` would expose Host-local policy metadata on model-provider and durable diagnostic paths. A second policy registry would have to reproduce prompt and tool resolution and could disagree about the contribution that actually won.
+
+## Decision
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-system-prompt` owns the branded `HostSourceId` vocabulary. `hostSourceIdForEntry()` derives an identity from the Loader tree namespace and the entry's full configured id; file-backed tree names are SHA-256 hashed so the identifier distinguishes trees without disclosing a configuration path. Registration outside a Loader entry uses the explicit `UNKNOWN_HOST_SOURCE_ID`. The identity stays stable across fiber replacement, row implementation replacement, and process restart while the tree filename and configured entry id remain the same.
+
+Prompt sections, contexts, and tool providers capture their source in the same scoped registry record as the contribution. `promptAssemblySourceInventory()` reads the sources of the effective assembly entries from a process-local `WeakMap`; source metadata adds no enumerable or serialized field. A waterfall listener that creates or copies an entry produces an unknown source, while a top-level assembly spread retains the marked entry objects. An admitted `complete` section is copied with its source before the waterfall and restored with that source afterward.
+
+`ToolProviderResult.sourceIds` optionally supplies one source for each returned schema. Omission attributes every schema to the provider registration; a present array must match the schema array exactly. `dsh-tools` captures the source beside every `ToolDefinition` and resolves the definition and source through the same shadowing path. Existing restriction semantics remain intact: restrictions filter inherited global and ancestor entries but not the viewing scope's own registrations, and the reserved `run_code` transport remains outside capability restrictions. Native, PTC, and combined projections pass one source per schema without changing any tool wire field.
+
+`SystemPrompt.admitSources()` registers a synchronous predicate in the calling global or scoped layer. Assembly evaluates every global and matching scope-chain predicate for every effective section, context, and tool candidate; all predicates run and all must accept. Section and context admission occurs after name shadowing but before text-provider evaluation and complete-section selection. Tool admission occurs per schema after a dynamic provider returns the schema and its source. Variables remain named interpolation inputs rather than independently model-visible assembly entries; admission applies to the section that consumes a variable. The assembly waterfall remains authoritative for extensions; its new entries are explicitly unknown and the post-assembly inventory lets a Host apply its final policy.
+
+## Verification
+
+The alpha.2 Host typecheck covers the source-aware system-prompt and tools registries together. Cordis catalog generation projects the new types and service method from their maintained source. Runtime behavior requires installation-level request-header evidence from the package that selects and applies source admission.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Add a `source` field to prompt and tool values.** Rejected because Host admission metadata is same-process policy state, not model-provider input or a durable protocol fact. A wire field would require every adapter and recorder to strip or understand it.
+
+**Identify a source by plugin package name.** Rejected because package names do not distinguish repeated configured rows, nested entry paths, or a stable row whose implementation reloads.
+
+**Maintain a Host-owned mirror of prompt and tool registrations.** Rejected because scoped shadowing, inherited restrictions, PTC transport insertion, dynamic schema projection, and complete-section restoration already have authoritative owners. Mirroring them creates a second resolution path.
+
+**Stop evaluating predicates after the first rejection.** Rejected because independently registered policies may need to observe every candidate, and predicate order must not change their observations. Admission remains conjunctive without short-circuiting.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Host policy can classify configured rows before they mount, admit their eventual model contributions through ordinary scoped effects, and inspect the exact post-waterfall assembly without changing provider requests. Provenance is intentionally process-local: serializing and reconstructing it would turn deployment composition into a durable model-input format.
+
+The source identity changes when a configuration tree moves to another filename or a configured entry id changes. Those operations define a different Host source and require policy to classify the new entry. Contributions created outside Loader composition and entries synthesized by assembly listeners are unknown, so a fail-closed Host must explicitly reject or allow that category.
+
+The system-prompt package gains a narrow dependency on the shared branding library, and the tools registry stores one additional branded value per registration. These costs replace plugin-specific ownership mirrors and keep prompt and tool resolution as the single authority.
